It��s been a few weeks since Pure Digital Technologies of San Francisco released its Flip Mino, the latest offering in its line of simple digital camcorders, which now account for almost 20 percent of the camcorder market.
I��ve been playing with it wondering where this would fit into people��s lives and how useful it would be over, say my Canon camera. The $179 Mino looks similar to its $149 predecessor the Ultra, though it��s smaller and thinner and comes with some nice improvements, like an integrated USB connector that flips out from the top of the device.
I broke it out at the Apple World Wide Developers Conference to capture some video and do a quick interview with an analyst. The picture quality was good and the audio is supposedly improved from the Ultra.
