Radio giant Cumulus Media Inc. has filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy protection to reduce more than $1 billion in debt.
Variety reports the company, which owns hundreds of U.S. radio stations including in Syracuse and Buffalo, announced Wednesday that it has entered into a restructuring agreement with more than two-thirds of its term loan holders. The filing took place at United States Bankruptcy Court for the Southern District of New York.
According to Reuters, Cumulus expects all radio operations, programming and sales to continue as normal throughout the restructuring process. It did not say if any layoffs are expected.
Cumulus currently owns more than a dozen radio stations in New York state, including NewsTalkRadio 77 WABC (WABC-AM) and 95.5 PLJ (WPLJ-FM) in NYC; 103.3 The Edge (WEDG-FM) and 97 Rock (WGRF-FM) in Buffalo; and 93Q (WNTQ-FM), 95X (WAQX-FM), The Score 1260 (WSKO-AM) and The Rebel 105.9 (WXTL-FM) in Syracuse.
Cumulus is the second-largest radio station owner in America behind iHeartMedia, formerly known as Clear Channel, which owns more than 30 stations across New York state. The parent company of iHeartRadio recently said there was "substantial doubt" about its ability to continue operating next year due to $20 billion in debt.
