Megyn Kelly is ready for bigger audiences.
The Syracuse native and Syracuse University alumna got her own prime time show on Fox News called "The Kelly File" in 2013. She's become a hot topic in the 2016 presidential election, she beats No. 1 personality Bill O'Reilly in ratings on the most-watched cable news network from time to time, and now she's hosting her own TV special.
And barely a decade ago, she wasn't even working in television.
Kelly steps into a new spotlight Tuesday night with "Megyn Kelly Presents," airing at 8 p.m. on the Fox broadcast network. The event, similar to broadcast legend Barbara Walters' "Most Fascinating People," features interviews with "Orange is the New Black" star Laverne Cox, actor Michael Douglas, O.J. Simpson lawyer Robert Shapiro, and presumptive Republican presidential nominee Donald Trump.
"I'm not Barbara," Kelly told the Associated Press last week. "But this is an opportunity to have a different kind of conversation than I'm used to."
The conversation everyone's looking forward to the most is her sit-down with Trump, who publicly feuded with Kelly after she asked him about alleged sexist remarks at the first GOP debate in August. Trump continued to hurl insults at the "lightweight" reporter, retweeting supporters who called her a "bimbo" and even suggesting she had it out for him from the start because she had "blood coming out of her wherever."
Kelly approached Trump last month for the interview after his attacks quieted down, and the billionaire changed his tune.
"I have great respect for you that you were able to call me and say, 'Let's get together, and let's talk.' To me, I would not have done that," Trump said in a segment that was previewed last week.
In promoting the special, Kelly has appeared everywhere: "Good Morning America" on ABC, "Late Night" with Stephen Colbert on CBS, "The Tonight Show" with Jimmy Fallon on NBC, "Watch What Happens Live" with Andy Cohen on Bravo, and "Live! with Kelly and Michael" (just before Strahan's departure) on ABC.
But Trump and the TV special are just her latest steps towards the top.
Kelly first started making a name for herself by hosting the afternoon show "America Live" on Fox News for three years. She also began co-hosting the network's New Year's Eve special in 2007, co-anchored the 2012 Republican National Convention, and landed big-name interviews with the likes of Mitt Romney, Facebook's Sheryl Sandberg and the Duggar family of "19 Kids and Counting" fame.
Her name came up more frequently on social media as she hit guests with harder questions, tackled controversial topics (including insisting Santa Claus is "white") and famously shut down Karl Rove's denial that Barack Obama had won the state of Ohio on election night in 2012. She also co-moderated three Republican presidential primary debates in the past year.
Kelly appeared in Variety, GQ, Vanity Fair and People magazines, even being named one of Time's 100 most influential people in 2014. She's stepped out at more red carpet events, including the Oscars with her husband Doug Brunt, the Met Gala and the 2016 Kentucky Derby.
And she never backed down from Trump, despite receiving death threats from his supporters in the past year.
"People are bullied when they're 55," Trump told Kelly when asked if he was ever bullied as a child.
"Can happen when you're 45," she responded with a smile. (Kelly is 45.)
"You know, it happens, right?" Trump continued. "But you gotta get over it. Fight back, do whatever you have to do."
Kelly continues to do whatever she has to do to succeed in television, but she almost didn't.
The 42-year-old broadcaster was born in Syracuse and attended Tecumseh Elementary School in the Jamesville-DeWitt school district. When she was nine years old, her family moved to the Albany area, but she later returned to Central New York to pursue a career in broadcast journalism at Syracuse University.
"I wanted to go to [the S.I.] Newhouse [School of Public Communications] but I didn't get in," Kelly told syracuse.com in 2013. "So I went over to the Maxwell School, which is also a great school, and decided to major in political science [and] I took a bunch of classes over at Newhouse."
But while at Maxwell, she found she enjoyed political science and changed course for a career in law. She went to Albany Law School and became a lawyer, practicing law for eight or nine years.
She later decided to give broadcast journalism another shot and landed a reporter job at Washington, D.C.'s ABC News affiliate WJLA-TV. She covered the Beltway sniper attacks and the 2004 presidential election before getting hired by Roger Ailes at Fox News.
