One of the interesting features of nativity plays this year is the number of them which tell the Christmas story through the perspective of ancillary characters. In The Innkeeper's Christmas, a grumpy innkeeper is woken up by successive visitors: Mary and Joseph, the three kings, the shepherds, a chorus of angels, etc.
In the production I saw, my son channelled Al Murray's pub landlord as the innkeeper. For added verisimilitude (kind of), he wore a Muslim skullcap I'd carried back from Socotra, having bought it off a man who told a long and complicated tale about a US drone that had landed on that island. Socotra lies in the Arabian Sea, dropped like a roc's egg between Yemen and Somalia.
In The Soldiers' Christmas, another nativity play I saw this week, the focus is on Roman soldiers of occupation. And there are hundreds of other versions out there, in which the focal perspective shifts – to the donkey accompanying the holy family, or the animals in the manger, even the star of Bethlehem above, looking down and commenting sarcastically, like the Greek god Hermes in John Banville's wonderful novel The Infinities.
Probably there is no particular cultural shift that can account for the popularity of this mode of storytelling. The search for inventive ways of telling the tale of Christ's birth has been going on a long time; in a way, difference was there from the start with Luke and Matthew.
It's tempting to speculate that this is what is happening to narrative in our age, a kind of splintering of the grand récit, or at least its diffusion into an array of loosely connected narrative cells. But the Greeks were at this for centuries, and Hindus still are.
In any culture, if information is to maximise in a contextual space, and new meanings be born, the original story has to have substance – there's gotta be gold in them thar hills. It is a mark of such classic narratives that they should be, in the critic Frank Kermode's immortal words, "patient of interpretation".
